# ==== Discord ====
DISCORD_TOKEN=__PUT_YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN_HERE__
GUILD_ID=__GUILD_ID__
ROLL_CHANNEL_ID=__CHANNEL_ID_FOR_ROLLS__
TRADE_CHANNEL_ID=__CHANNEL_ID_FOR_TRADES__
DT_CHANNEL_ID=__CHANNEL_ID_FOR_DOWNTIME__
BASTION_CHANNEL_ID=__CHANNEL_ID_FOR_BASTION__

# ==== Google Sheets ====
# ID der Google-Tabelle (die lange ID in der URL)
GUILD_SHEET_ID=__GOOGLE_SHEET_ID__

# Service-Account-Creds als EINZEILIGE JSON-Zeichenkette.
# Tipp: Doppelte Anführungszeichen bleiben, Newlines im private_key als \n.
# Beispiel-Form (PLATZHALTER!):
# SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON='{"type":"service_account","project_id":"...","private_key_id":"...","private_key":"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nABC...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n","client_email":"bot@proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com","client_id":"123","token_uri":"https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token"}'
SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON='__MINIFIED_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON__'

# Optional, and read only by tools/import_sheet_characters.py -- never by the
# bot. A SECOND service account holding only Viewer on the spreadsheet, used
# with the .../auth/spreadsheets.readonly scope, so that an import run cannot
# write to the Sheet even by mistake. Leave unset and the importer falls back
# to SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON above and warns that it did.
# See docs/operations/sheet-import.md.
# SHEET_READONLY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON='__MINIFIED_READONLY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON__'

# ==== PostgreSQL (Phase 1+) ====
# Not read by the Discord bot. Phase 1 wires these to migrations and tests only.
# Development and production must use distinct databases and restricted roles.
# The running application role must not own the schema or run migrations.
#
# Selects which database name is permitted: development|test|staging|production.
# Defaults to development, so an unqualified migration can only reach freedom_dev.
#
# Migrations and the destructive database tests require the local PostgreSQL
# Unix-domain socket: no host in the URL (as below), or ?host=/var/run/postgresql.
# A TCP URL is refused for them, loopback included, because 127.0.0.1:5432 can be
# an SSH local port forward to another host's PostgreSQL and the server at the
# far end reports loopback addresses just as a local one does.
#
# Keep PGHOSTADDR unset. libpq dials `hostaddr` and keeps `host` only for
# authentication, so PGHOSTADDR turns either socket spelling above into a TCP
# connection to that address -- and a remote one would receive these credentials.
# The combination is refused before anything connects. See
# docs/operations/database-development.md.
APP_ENVIRONMENT=development
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://__APP_USER__:__PASSWORD__@/__DATABASE__
# Tests require a disposable PostgreSQL database containing synthetic data only.
# It must differ from DATABASE_URL: the integration tests drop every table.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://__TEST_USER__:__PASSWORD__@/__TEST_DATABASE__

# ==== Foundry snapshot submission (Phase 2 I-03) ====
# Read only by the snapshot submission endpoint -- never by the Discord bot.
# See docs/operations/foundry-snapshot-submission.md.
#
# Absolute directory, OUTSIDE this repository, holding the immutable snapshot
# artifacts. There is deliberately no default: every default would be a guess
# about which filesystem holds every exported Actor's mechanics. It is created
# 0700 and each artifact 0600, named for its own SHA-256 and never for anything
# a caller supplied. No application route serves an artifact and there is no
# directory listing; retention and deletion are operator actions.
#
# The state is ENFORCED, at startup and on every store and read, and never
# repaired: it must be a real path with no symlinked component, a directory
# owned by the service account, granting nothing to group or other, on a
# filesystem that can fsync a directory. The service refuses to start otherwise,
# naming a fixed reason and never the path. See §5.6 of the operations document.
#
# THE FILESYSTEM MUST SUPPORT HARD LINKS. Publication creates the artifact's
# directory entry with link(2), which fails rather than overwriting an entry the
# service has not validated -- that is what stops a concurrent or unexpected file
# under a checksum name being silently destroyed. ext4, XFS, Btrfs, ZFS and tmpfs
# are fine; FAT/exFAT and some network mounts are not. Startup proves it and
# refuses `link_unsupported` if it does not hold.
#
# One consequence for whoever sizes this filesystem: publication writes a
# `.incoming-*` temporary, links the checksum name to it, and then unlinks the
# temporary BEST EFFORT. The service swallows a failure there rather than
# failing a submission whose artifact is already published, so it does not
# report one -- and a leftover `.incoming-*` link can survive a SUCCESSFUL store
# as well as a failed one. It is private (0600, in a 0700 root), no route serves
# it, and it can never invalidate or delete the published artifact; it consumes
# storage until an operator removes it. Operations §5.6, "Temporary files left
# by a failed cleanup", is the read-only procedure for finding them. Repeated
# leftovers mean the unlink is failing -- usually a drifted root mode or owner,
# or a full or read-only filesystem.
#
# The DIRECTORIES ABOVE IT are checked too, at startup: each must be owned by
# root or by the service account and must not be writable by others unless it
# carries the sticky bit. That is what stops another account renaming the store
# out from under the service. Check yours with `namei -l <path>` before
# deploying; the usual root:root 0755 chain satisfies it.
#
# The root is opened once and every later operation is resolved against that
# descriptor, so replacing the path while the service runs redirects nothing --
# but it is also refused (root_replaced) rather than ignored. MOVING OR
# REPLACING THE ARTIFACT ROOT THEREFORE REQUIRES A SERVICE RESTART.
FREEDOM_SNAPSHOT_ARTIFACT_ROOT=/srv/freedom/snapshots

# The credentials the endpoint accepts. One entry per principal, ';'-separated:
#
#     <id>|<scope>[,<scope>]|<sha256 of the secret, hex>
#
# This holds the DIGEST of the secret, never the secret -- so compromise of this
# file yields no usable credential. Generate a secret and its digest with the
# commands in docs/operations/foundry-snapshot-submission.md §5.2, give the
# secret to the submitting GM for their password manager, and put only the
# digest here.
#
# The secret is NOT stored in Foundry. A world setting's value is delivered to
# every client that joins the world, so the submitting GM enters it in the
# dialog once per submission. Operations document §4.1 records the Foundry
# source that establishes this.
#
# The only scope that exists is foundry:snapshot:submit. It permits uploading a
# snapshot and nothing else: not applying an import, not reading Council data,
# not mutating a character, not reaching PostgreSQL.
#
# Leaving this unset or empty is a valid configuration -- every request then
# fails authentication, which is also how "revoke everything" is spelled.
# FREEDOM_SNAPSHOT_PRINCIPALS='foundry-the-guild|foundry:snapshot:submit|__SHA256_HEX_OF_THE_SECRET__'

# The browser origins permitted to submit a snapshot. Comma- or
# whitespace-separated, EXACT origins only -- the scheme and host a browser
# actually sends, lower-case, with the port only when it is not the scheme's
# default. No wildcard, no suffix match, and no reflection of whatever arrived.
#
# The Foundry module submits from a browser with four non-safelisted headers, so
# the browser sends an unauthenticated OPTIONS preflight first and will not send
# the POST unless it is answered. This is the allowlist that answers it.
#
# Leaving this unset or empty is a valid configuration and the default: NO
# browser origin may submit. A caller that sends no Origin -- curl, the
# documented smoke test, the loopback rehearsal -- is unaffected either way.
# Malformed entries are refused at startup. See §5.4 of the operations document.
# FREEDOM_SNAPSHOT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS='https://foundry1.example.org,https://foundry2.example.org'
